{"availability":"available","full":"christianhbye/croissant","name":"croissant","description":"A differentiable visibility simulator based on spherical harmonics","category":"Other","language":"Jupyter Notebook","stars":5,"forks":2,"created":"2022-05-09T22:00:11Z","updated":"2026-10-09T22:53:42Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"","topics":["astronomy","differentiable","jax","radio-astronomy","simulator","spherical-harmonics"],"demo":"https://nbviewer.org/github/christianhbye/croissant/blob/main/notebooks/example_sim.ipynb","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T03:20:42.064Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-10T03:20:42.064Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-10T03:20:42.064Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-07T23:31:41.985Z","stars":5,"forks":2},{"at":"2026-10-10T03:20:42.064Z","stars":5,"forks":2}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["CROISSANT is a rapid visiblity simulator in python based on spherical harmonics. Given an antenna design and a sky model, CROISSANT simulates the visbilities - that is, the perceived sky temperature.","CROISSANT uses spherical harmonics to decompose the sky and antenna beam to a set of coefficients. Since the spherical harmonics represents a complete, orthormal basis on the sphere, the visibility computation reduces nicely from a convolution to a dot product.","Moreover, the time evolution of the simulation is very natural in this representation. In the antenna reference frame, the sky rotates overhead with time. To account for this rotation, it is enough to rotate the spherical harmonics coefficients. In the right choice of coordinates (that is, one where the z-axis is aligned with the rotation axis of the earth or the moon), this rotation is simply achieved by multiplying the spherical coefficient by a phase."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/christianhbye/croissant/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"e765cd5f60895d236d127bc9bbb5f554d9601a223d2c284d262b631f7fe06ec9","e